.\" swapspace manpage
.TH SWAPSPACE 8 "October 2026" "swapspace 1.19" "Linux System Administration"
.SH NAME
swapspace \- dynamically manage swap space
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B swapspace
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Monitor memory and swap usage and allocate or deallocate swap space as needed.
This program aims to reduce or do away with the need for swap partitions.
.PP
.B swapspace
can be built in two configurations: the full-featured one (which is
now the default) or the unconfigurable version.  The latter only accepts a few
options, disabling all features that are probably only relevant to developers.
.PP
The following options are accepted in the unconfigurable version.  All of these
have more extensive equivalents in the full-featured version.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR
Quietly ignored; always enabled in unconfigurable version.
.TP
\fB\-e\fR
Attempt to free up all allocated swap files.  Returns 0 if all files were
successfully erased, or 1 otherwise.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
Display usage information and exit.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR
Write process identifier to \fI/var/run/swapspace.pid\fR when starting (and
delete it when shutting down).  The file's name and location cannot be changed
in the unconfigurable version.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR
Quietly ignored; always enabled in unconfigurable version.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR
Quietly ignored; always enabled in unconfigurable version.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
Print program version information and exit.
.PP
In most cases, these should be the only options of any interest for normal use
of the program.  All files are kept in their default locations, which were
chosen in accordance with the Linux Filesystem Hierarchy Standard, and
algorithmic parameters are left at defaults that have been tested to work well
for a wide range of uses.
.PP
The full-featured configuration accepts more options, as listed below.
The long-format options may also be specified, without the leading "\-\-", in a
configuration file.  By default, \fI/etc/swapspace.conf\fR is read on startup.
.TP
\fB\-a\fR \fIduration\fR, \fB\-\-cooldown\fR=\fIduration\fR
If disk space runs out when allocating a swapfile, wait for \fIduration\fR
iterations (of roughly one second each) before considering allocating one
again; or if space doesn't run out, wait for \fIduration\fR iterations before 
considering deallocating unneeded swapfiles.  This stabilizes the daemon's
behaviour in the face of varying memory requirements.
.TP
\fB\-B\fR \fIp\fR, \fB\-\-buffer_elasticity\fR=\fIp\fR
Consider \fIp\fR% of system-allocated I/O buffers to be available for other use.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR \fIfile\fR, \fB\-\-configfile\fR=\fIfile\fR
Read \fIfile\fR instead of the default configuration file.
.TP
\fB\-C\fR \fIp\fR, \fB\-\-cache_elasticity\fR=\fIp\fR
Consider \fIp\fR% of filesystem cache to be available for other use.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-daemon\fR
Run quietly in the background.  This is the normal way to run the program.
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-erase\fR
Attempt to free up all allocated swap files.  Returns 0 if all files were
successfully erased, or 1 otherwise.
.TP
\fB\-E\fR, \fB\-\-crypt\fR
Encrypt swapfiles.  Each new swapfile is attached to a loop device and mapped
through dm-crypt (plain mode) with a random key read from \fI/dev/urandom\fR,
and swap is made on and enabled from the mapped device
\fI/dev/mapper/swapspace\-N\fR instead of from the file.  The key exists only in
the kernel and is gone when the mapping is removed.  Off by default.  If any
step fails, nothing is enabled and the file is deleted (kept only if a mapping
that still holds it cannot be removed, until it can be): a swapfile is never
activated unencrypted while this option is on.  See \fBENCRYPTED SWAPFILES\fR
for what this does and does not protect, and its requirements.
.TP
\fB\-K\fR \fIbits\fR, \fB\-\-crypt_keysize\fR=\fIbits\fR
Key size in bits for \fB\-\-crypt\fR; default 512, range 128 to 1024, a multiple
of 8.  XTS ciphers (the default) split the key in two and need 256 or 512;
AES in other modes (including a bare \fIaes\fR) takes 128, 192 or 256.  Those
sizes are checked at startup.  For any other cipher only the range is checked:
cryptsetup or the kernel refuses an unsuitable size when the first mapping is
created, and then nothing is enabled.
.TP
\fB\-X\fR \fIcipher\fR, \fB\-\-crypt_cipher\fR=\fIcipher\fR
Cipher specification for \fB\-\-crypt\fR, in cryptsetup's notation; default
\fIaes\-xts\-plain64\fR.  Because it is passed to a shell command line it may
contain only letters, digits and the characters ":_.\-", and must start with a
letter or digit; anything else is rejected at startup.  Ciphers that give no
confidentiality are rejected too: any name containing \fInull\fR
(\fIcipher_null\fR stores plaintext) and the \fIecb\fR chain mode, which
leaves repeated blocks recognizable and so would show structure in swapped-out
memory.  Apart from that this only validates the spelling: the kernel decides
whether the cipher exists.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR \fIp\fR, \fB\-\-freetarget\fR=\fIp\fR
Aim to have \fIp\fR% of combined memory and swap space free.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Display usage information and exit.
.TP
\fB\-l\fR \fIp\fR, \fB\-\-lower_freelimit\fR=\fIp\fR
Try to keep at least \fIp\fR% of combined memory and swap space free; if less
than \fIp\fR percent is available, attempt to allocate more swap space.
.TP
\fB\-M\fR \fIsize\fR, \fB\-\-max_swapsize\fR=\fIsize\fR
Never let swapfiles become larger than \fIsize\fR bytes.  You don't normally
need to set this; the daemon will learn when its swap files get too big and
adapt automatically.
.TP
\fB\-m\fR \fIsize\fR, \fB\-\-min_swapsize\fR=\fIsize\fR
Never bother to allocate any swapfiles smaller than \fIsize\fR bytes.  There
should be no need to change this variable except for testing.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR [\fIfile\fR], \fB\-\-pidfile\fR[=\fIfile\fR]
Write process identifier to \fIfile\fR when starting (and delete \fIfile\fR when
shutting down); defaults to \fI/var/run/swapspace.pid\fR.
.TP
\fB\-P\fR, \fB\-\-paranoid\fR
Overwrite retired swapfiles before they are deleted, so an attacker who obtains
access to the disk without going through the system's access control checks
(e.g. by unplugging the computer and then rebooting from a CD) cannot retrieve
the swapped data.  There is no guarantee that this will work, and it will not
thwart advanced forensic analysis using custom-built hardware; but it may reduce
the chances of an attacker with physical access to the system obtaining
passwords, credit card numbers etc.  The program will attempt to free up all
allocated swapfiles on termination and return a success code for this cleanup,
as if the \fB\-\-erase\fR had been specified.
.TP
\fBCaution\fR
The \fB\-\-paranoid\fR option will slow down swap file management considerably. 
In particular, stopping the daemon will cause it to try and deallocate (and
wipe) all swapfiles it has created, and they will not be available for swapping
immediately after reboot.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress informational output.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR \fIdir\fR, \fB\-\-swappath\fR=\fIdir\fR
Create swapfiles in directory \fIdir\fR instead of default location
\fI/var/lib/swapspace\fR.  This location must be accessible to \fIroot\fR only;
allowing anyone else to write to this directory or even read swapped data would
be a \fUserious security breach\fR.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR \fIp\fR, \fB\-\-upper_freelimit\fR=\fIp\fR
Avoid having more than \fIp\fR% of combined memory and swap space free; if this
percentage is exceeded, try to deallocate swap space.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Log debug information to system log and/or standard output, as appropriate.
.TP
\fB\-x\fR \fIp\fR, \fB\-\-psi_threshold\fR=\fIp\fR
Treat the system as under memory pressure when the kernel's Pressure Stall
Information reports a "some avg10" value above \fIp\fR percent, and avoid
freeing swap while under pressure.  Set to 0 to disable.  PSI requires Linux
4.20 or later; the option has no effect when \fI/proc/pressure/memory\fR is
unavailable.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print program version information and exit.
.PP
Numbers may be suffixed with \fIk\fR, \fIm\fR, \fIg\fR or \fIt\fR to indicate
kilobytes, megabytes, gigabytes or terabytes respectively: \fI1k\fR means 1024
bytes, \fI1m\fR means 1024 kilobytes, \fI4g\fR means 4096 megabytes and so on.
.PP
Timings are measured in \fIiterations\fR, which should typically last about one
second each.  No pretense of accurate timing is made; this is not the kind of
program you would run on a hard-realtime system.
.PP
Any messages are sent to the system daemon log; it is also printed to the
standard output/error streams (as appropriate based on the urgency of each
individual message) if available.
.SH ENCRYPTED SWAPFILES
With \fB\-\-crypt\fR, swapfiles are created as usual (with the \fBNOCOW\fR
attribute where supported), except that they are filled with zeros and flushed
instead of being preallocated: unwritten extents would need block allocation on
the first write, which is the wrong thing to need while swapping out.  They are
then set up as follows: the file is
attached to a free loop device with direct I/O, \fBcryptsetup open \-\-type
plain\fR maps it as \fIswapspace\-N\fR using a random key, \fBmkswap\fR is run on
\fI/dev/mapper/swapspace\-N\fR, and that device is enabled with \fBswapon\fR.
Retiring a swapfile runs \fBswapoff\fR, \fBcryptsetup close\fR, detaches the
loop device and deletes the file; \fB\-\-erase\fR and \fB\-\-paranoid\fR work
as before.  Active mappings named \fIswapspace\-N\fR are recognized in
\fI/proc/swaps\fR (as \fI/dev/mapper/swapspace\-N\fR or \fI/dev/dm\-X\fR) and
adopted on startup whether or not \fB\-\-crypt\fR is given, so they are retired
correctly after a configuration change.  A mapping is adopted only if the loop
device under it is backed by file N of this swap directory, which is checked
again on every read of \fI/proc/swaps\fR; one that is not (for example another
instance's) is left alone and reported once.  Mappings that are not backed by a
file of this directory are never closed, in any mode, and their slot numbers are
not used.
.PP
After a reboot the key of an old file is gone, so inactive files found at
startup cannot be reused in this mode.  They are deleted (overwritten first
with \fB\-\-paranoid\fR, since an old plain file may hold readable data) and
new ones are created when needed.  To switch from
plain to encrypted swapfiles cleanly, run \fBswapspace \-\-erase\fR (with
\fB\-\-paranoid\fR if you want the old data overwritten) with the old
configuration first.  A mapping left over from an interrupted setup is removed
at startup, or before its slot is used again.  Mapping names are global: run
only one instance with \fB\-\-crypt\fR per host.
.PP
\fBWhat this protects:\fR the contents of the swapfiles as stored on disk.
Someone who obtains the disk, a copy of it, or freed blocks of it cannot read
swapped-out memory, because the key was never written anywhere.
.PP
\fBWhat it does not protect:\fR memory itself, including everything that is not
swapped out; zram and zswap, which hold plaintext (or compressed plaintext) in
RAM; hibernation images; other swap devices; the data of a running system
against an attacker with root; or its integrity (this is encryption without
authentication).  File names, sizes and timing are visible.  The key passes
through the memory of the \fBcryptsetup\fR process once, when it is read from
\fI/dev/urandom\fR.
.PP
\fBRequirements:\fR \fBcryptsetup\fR 2.0 or later in the \fBPATH\fR (the
\fBopen\fR and \fBclose\fR subcommands, and \fB\-\-batch\-mode\fR), the loop and
dm-crypt kernel modules, a kernel with the \fBLOOP_CONFIGURE\fR loop ioctl
(Linux 5.8 or later, and kernel headers of that age when building), and a swap
directory on a filesystem and device where direct I/O (\fBO_DIRECT\fR) works
with the loop device's block size.  The block size is chosen for that: it is the
file's direct I/O alignment (\fBSTATX_DIOALIGN\fR, else the filesystem block
size), rounded up to a power of two, at least 512 bytes and at most the page
size, so that 4Kn storage works.  (Swap does its I/O in pages and cannot use a
device with larger logical blocks, so if a filesystem asked for more than that,
setup fails with a message saying that the alignment exceeds the page size.)  On \fBbtrfs\fR the swap files must
have \fBNOCOW\fR, or this mode refuses to create them: copy-on-write files get
their direct writes bounced through newly allocated memory.  Mirrored data
profiles (\fBDUP\fR, \fBRAID1\fR) can cause the same even with \fBNOCOW\fR;
use the \fBsingle\fR data profile for the filesystem holding the swap
directory.  Direct I/O is requested together with that
block size when the loop device is configured.  If the kernel does not enable it
(some filesystems cannot do direct I/O on files at all), setup fails with a
message saying so, rather than falling back to buffered I/O.
.PP
\fBCost:\fR every swap access passes through the loop driver and dm-crypt, which
costs CPU time and some kernel memory, and creating a swapfile takes a few extra
commands and writes the whole file instead of preallocating it.
.PP
\fBCaveat:\fR this mode trades robustness under extreme memory pressure for
encryption.  The loop driver does its I/O on a workqueue that is allocated
without \fBWQ_MEM_RECLAIM\fR, so it has no rescuer thread: when memory is too
short to start a worker, the writes that are supposed to free memory can wait
for memory and deadlock.  The same workqueue is freezable, so swap-out to the
device stalls while the system is suspending, until it resumes.  Direct I/O
(always used here) removes the backing file's page cache from the path, and
writing the file out in full removes block allocation from it (on \fBbtrfs\fR
only with \fBNOCOW\fR and a single data profile), but neither removes these
two properties.  If your system routinely runs this close to the
edge or suspends under memory pressure, prefer zram, swap on an encrypted
partition or another path that does not go through a loop device, or keep a
reserve of free memory.
.SH SIGNALS
State information can be logged by sending the program the \fBSIGUSR1\fR signal
(user-defined signal 1).  Not all of this information will always be current;
most of the information internal to \fBswapspace\fR is only updated when needed.
.PP
Sending the \fBSIGUSR2\fR signal will make the program free all swapfiles that
are not currently needed, and abstain from allocating any more for the timespan
of one cooldown period.  The program will behave as if it just tried to create a
swapfile but ran out of disk space.
.SH FILES
\& /etc/init.d/swapspace
\& /etc/swapspace.conf
\& /usr/sbin/swapspace
\& /var/lib/swapspace/
